# Origami file-format fixtures are byte-significant.
#
# They are compared against what the upstream tools produce, round-tripped
# through our writers, and ~50 of them are embedded directly into test binaries
# with `include_str!`. Git's end-of-line translation rewrites files on checkout
# on Windows (`core.autocrlf` is true by default there), so without this the same
# fixture is a different byte sequence depending on which machine ran the tests —
# which is exactly what a parity-focused port must not allow.
#
# This is not hypothetical. It is how
# `treemaker-core::tests::v3_fixed_angle_preserves_501_import_behavior` failed on
# the very first Windows CI run: the test builds its input with
# `replacen("false\n0.0000000000\ntrue", ...)`, the checked-out fixture had CRLF,
# the pattern matched nothing, the substitution silently did not happen, and the
# assertion reported `None` instead of `Some(1.0)`. The parser itself was fine —
# the sibling test that parses the same fixture passed.
*.tmd   -text
*.tmd4  -text
*.tmd5  -text
*.cp    -text
*.fold  -text
*.ori   -text
*.orh   -text
*.osf   -text
